Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(ArgRetired);
define
    arBefore code={ print "AR BEFORE ran":; };
    arBad argument code={ print "AR BAD ran -- a REFUSED definition was installed":; };
    arAfter code={ print "AR AFTER ran":; };
    ;
arBefore();
arBad();
arAfter();
print "ARGRETIRED SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

argRetiredT -- A DECLARED `argument` IS A RETIRED SPELLING AND REFUSES BY NAME.

runAction MINTS the binding slot, so there is nothing left to declare. The
retired spelling does not quietly do something slightly different: it says so,
names the respell, and the definition is NOT INSTALLED.

FOUR THINGS ASSERTED, and the three neighbours are what make the middle one
mean anything:

    AR BEFORE ran            the definition BEFORE the refusal is unharmed
    REFUSED arBad ...        the refusal fires, BY NAME, with the respell in it
    AR BAD absent            the refused definition was NOT installed
    AR AFTER ran             the definition AFTER the refusal is unharmed

⚠ `AR AFTER` IS THE ROW THAT COSTS SOMETHING TO GET WRONG, and it is here
because getting it wrong was measured. A refusal is terminal for THE UNIT THAT
RAISED IT (Tony, 2026-09-05): at run time that unit is the activation, at define
time it is the DEFINITION. A first attempt armed without clearing at the
definition boundary, so the first declared `argument` in a file killed EVERY
LATER DEFINITION IN IT -- fleet 171 -> 107. `AR AFTER` is the row that would
have caught it.

⚠ AND `AR BAD` IS AN ABSENCE, SO IT DOES NOT STAND ALONE (H4). It is read only
alongside the sentinel and the two neighbours: sentinel present plus BEFORE and
AFTER present means the run completed and BAD was SKIPPED; sentinel absent would
mean the run died, which is a different fact. Without that pairing the row would
pass on any truncated run.

ONE STOP(), sentinel at the foot.
